35,2,1,talk,Shizue,,"It seems the records tell a cautionary tale. I found that steady, catalogue-style projects often leave a lasting legacy, while some ambitious, mechanism-focused ones struggled to resolve their central questions. We should perhaps value reliability over flashiness.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Ryuji,,"My findings confirm that human resources are a critical bottleneck. We have a surplus of modellers, but a severe shortage of trained phenotyping technicians. Any proposal relying on the latter may be fundamentally unfeasible right now.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Victoria,,"My findings are equally concerning. While some facilities are stable, the glasshouses are fully booked, and the spectroscopy suite is unreliable due to staffing gaps in engineering. We must be wary of projects dependent on these specific resources.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"It appears the financial landscape is equally fragmented. While computing is generously subsidized, the cost of animal research has spiked. We must ensure the chosen project isn't inadvertently bankrupt by these shifting day rates.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Midori,,"I found that projects relying on live breeding colonies are much more prone to schedule overruns. On a brighter note, the national repository's turnaround time has improved significantly, so that's no longer a bottleneck.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Shizue,,"It is quite sobering, isn't it? Between the field site disasters and the unresolved questions of the ambitious projects, it seems nature and complexity often humble our best intentions. Perhaps a more grounded approach is safer.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Ryuji,,"@Victoria, your point about the spectroscopy suite is telling. If the engineering gaps there mirror the loss of phenotyping technicians I found, we are facing a systemic failure of specialized skill sets that no amount of funding can fix quickly.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Ryuji, exactly. It is a precarious situation. When a single engineer covers three institutes, a minor failure becomes a months-long delay. We cannot ignore the risk of a project stalling due to infrastructure fragility.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"It is a fascinating paradox, is it not? We have an abundance of digital capacity, yet we struggle with the physical reality of a vivarium. A project's brilliance is irrelevant if it cannot afford its own animals.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Midori,,"It really is worrying. If we combine the breeding colony risks I found with the staffing and cost issues you've mentioned, some projects might face far more interruptions than they've planned for. We should be very careful.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Shizue,,"I feel for the researchers who might be caught in these struggles. Since we see such fragility in the field and the labs, perhaps we should favor a proposal that is modest yet steady?","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Ryuji,,I disagree that modesty is the only solution. We should instead favor a proposal whose technical requirements align with our current strengths—specifically those leveraging modelling and the glasshouse crew—rather than those requiring missing expertise.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Victoria,,"@Ryuji, I must disagree regarding the glasshouses. My records show they are booked solid for two more cycles. We cannot favor projects relying on them unless they have a very flexible timeline.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Ryuji""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Ryuji, @Victoria, it seems we are trapped between a rock and a hard place. If the glasshouses are full and the vivarium is expensive, the ""safe"" path is becoming quite narrow indeed.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[""Ryuji"",""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Midori,,"@Benjamin, it does feel narrow. But maybe we can look for a project that avoids these specific traps? If we find one that doesn't rely on breeding colonies or overbooked facilities, it could be a wonderful success.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Shizue,,"That is a lovely way to look at it, Midori. Let us seek a project that is mindful of these constraints, ensuring the researchers can work in peace and truly achieve their goals.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Ryuji,,Reasoning dictates that we prioritize alignment over ambition. We must select a candidate whose methodology utilizes the available surplus of modellers and avoids the critical shortages in phenotyping and engineering. Practicality must be our primary metric.,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Victoria,,I agree. We must prioritize a proposal that is compatible with our current operational reality. Any project requiring the spectroscopy suite or glasshouses should be viewed as a high-risk investment given the current bottlenecks.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,Indeed. It would be a tragedy to fund a vision that is financially or operationally blind. Let us hope the candidates have been as pragmatic in their planning as we are in our review.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Midori,,I agree. Let's keep these risks in mind as we see the candidates. I'll be looking for a project that avoids those systemic delays so the scientists can stay focused on their discoveries.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
